#{[17592186542161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542161 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542161 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution of ${5}^{x+3}={2}^{5-4x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "708af368-563d-5975-a829-b0da728a3e85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542161 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+3} & ={2}^{5-4x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5-4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(5-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(5-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-242905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542165 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-3}=5\\cdot {8}^{2x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c789237-199d-5f09-befa-8f93e41fb414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542165 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & =5\\cdot {8}^{2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {8}^{2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {8}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x}) \\\\ x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542197 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542197 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542197 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542197 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x+1}={3}^{x+2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542197 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b722f56c-7d4f-5883-9de4-4bfad6f8a324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542197 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =(x+2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542209 #edb/ref 1069 "q-242913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542209 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542209 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${4}^{3x-1}={9}^{x+6}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce76cbe9-5155-5439-bee7-2d4f7a68d153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542209 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3x-1} & ={9}^{x+6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(3x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)=(x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542214 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542214 #edb/ref 1069 "q-242901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542214 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542214 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{2x-1}=3\\cdot {8}^{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0454be3-0e05-5ea2-b188-0b8e56e969a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542214 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =3\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {8}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {8}^{x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{x}) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+1 \\\\ 2x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1 \\\\ x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1}{2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
